        <h1>Water–ethanol mixtures: strength, density and correct dilution</h1>
        <p class="rehber-ozet">
            When alcohol and water are mixed, the volumes do not add up: the mixture occupies less space than
            its components did separately. Dilutions made by "simple proportion" therefore always give a little
            less mixture than expected, and a slightly stronger one. This page is both the user manual and the
            theoretical background of the <a href="/alcohol-dilution">Alcohol Dilution</a> tool, which is based
            on the equation of the International Alcoholometric Tables (OIML R 22).
        </p>
