    <section id="giris">
        <h2>Introduction</h2>
        <p>
            Ethanol is the solvent diluted most often in the pharmacy and the pharmaceutical technology
            laboratory. Tinctures and extracts call for an extraction solvent of a given strength; skin and
            surface antisepsis uses 70 % alcohol; compounded prescriptions, solutions and granulation liquids
            need hydroalcoholic mixtures of various strengths. What is on the shelf, however, is usually 96 % or
            absolute alcohol. The task is always the same: to prepare a given amount of mixture at a given
            strength from a stock of known strength.
        </p>
        <p>
            The familiar "C<sub>1</sub>V<sub>1</sub> = C<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>" of aqueous solutions is
            misleading here. Even when the stock volume is right, adding water to make up the missing volume
            makes the mixture contract: the target volume is not reached and the strength ends up slightly above
            the target. A correct calculation treats <strong>mass</strong>, not volume, as the conserved quantity
            and takes the densities, including their temperature dependence, from an internationally agreed
            equation. This guide covers the concepts first, then the OIML equation and tables, and finally the
            numerical methods used by the tool and the dilution calculation itself.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="tanimlar">
        <h2>Definitions</h2>
        <p>
            OIML R 22 defines the "alcoholic strength" of a water–ethanol mixture in two ways (OIML, 1975):
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Alcoholic strength by volume</strong> (% v/v, % vol, q): the volume of alcohol at 20 °C
                contained in the mixture divided by the total volume of the mixture at 20 °C.</li>
            <li><strong>Alcoholic strength by mass</strong> (% m/m, % w/w, p): the mass of alcohol contained in the
                mixture divided by the total mass of the mixture.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Strength by volume depends on temperature, which is why the reference temperature is fixed explicitly
            at 20 °C. Strength by mass does not: warming or cooling a mixture leaves the masses unchanged. The
            two are related through the densities at 20 °C:
        </p>
        <p class="rehber-formul">
            φ = w · ρ(w, 20 °C) / ρ<sub>E</sub>(20 °C)
        </p>
        <p>
            Here w is the mass fraction, φ the strength by volume (as a fraction), ρ(w, 20 °C) the density of the
            mixture and ρ<sub>E</sub>(20 °C) that of pure ethanol at 20 °C (789.24 kg/m³). The denominator in the
            definition is the volume of the <em>mixture</em>, not the sum of the volumes of water and alcohol
            before mixing. This is not a mere subtlety: mixing 50 mL of alcohol with 50 mL of water does not give
            50 % v/v but something slightly stronger, because the denominator has shrunk. Meija (2009) uses the
            "vodka challenge" attributed to Mendeleev to show why volume concentration and the volume fraction
            before mixing give different numbers.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="buzulme">
        <h2>Volume contraction</h2>
        <p>
            When water and ethanol are mixed, the volume change on mixing (the excess volume, V<sup>E</sup>) is
            <strong>negative</strong> across the whole composition range: the mixture is smaller than the sum of
            the volumes its components occupied separately. The molecular reasons are explained in
            <a href="/about-alcohol-dilution#neden">the next section</a>. The excess volume has been measured in detail at
            several temperatures and by different methods (Benson &amp; Kiyohara, 1980; Marsh &amp; Richards, 1980;
            Grolier &amp; Wilhelm, 1981). Computed from the OIML equation, the molar excess volume at 20 °C reaches
            its minimum of about −1.1 cm³/mol near an ethanol mole fraction of 0.4.
        </p>
        <p>
            A more intuitive measure for the pharmacist is the percentage of the mixed volumes that "disappears".
            This relative contraction is largest at about 46–47 % alcohol by mass, a composition corresponding
            roughly to 1 mol of ethanol to 3 mol of water, which was the starting point of Mendeleev's hydrate
            interpretations. There, about 3.5 % of the mixed volumes is lost. A practical example: mixing 50 mL of
            absolute alcohol with 50 mL of water at 20 °C yields about 96.5 mL of mixture.
        </p>
        <p>
            The contraction also changes somewhat with temperature (Marsh &amp; Richards, 1980), so a correct
            calculation relies not on a single "contraction factor" but on a density equation that depends on both
            composition and temperature.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="neden">
        <h2>Why does the volume shrink?</h2>
        <p>
            There is no single cause of the contraction. Several complementary molecular effects act together, and
            their relative weight is still being studied. Experimental and theoretical work converges on three of
            them.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>1. The open network of water and hydrophobic hydration.</strong> In liquid water each molecule
            forms a roughly tetrahedral hydrogen-bond network with its neighbours. This network is open and full of
            voids relative to the size of the molecules, which is also why ice is lighter than water. When a little
            ethanol is added, its non-polar ethyl group is accommodated within the network and the surrounding water
            molecules rearrange around it (hydrophobic hydration). As a result, ethanol in dilute solution occupies
            clearly less than its molar volume in the pure liquid (≈58 cm³/mol): at an ethanol mole fraction of 0.038
            the partial molar excess volume of ethanol is about −5.2 cm³/mol (Marsh &amp; Richards, 1980).
            Simulations explain this drop in the partial molar volume of ethanol by the hydration shells of the ethyl
            groups beginning to touch one another (Tan et al., 2015). As a rough analogy, when sand is poured into a
            bucket of marbles, part of the sand fills the gaps and the total volume stays below the sum of the two.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>2. Water–ethanol hydrogen bonds.</strong> The hydroxyl group of ethanol hydrogen-bonds with
            water. X-ray absorption spectroscopy has shown that the hydrogen bonding originating from the ethanol OH
            group increases as water is added (Lam et al., 2016). Molecular dynamics simulations with polarisable
            models likewise found that the water–water bonds lost in the first hydration shell of ethanol are more
            than compensated in the second shell, so that up to an ethanol mole fraction of about 0.5 the mixture
            contains more hydrogen bonds in total than the pure liquids (Noskov et al., 2005). More bonds bring the
            molecules closer together on average. That mixing releases heat, i.e. that the enthalpy of mixing is
            negative, is consistent with this picture (Boyne &amp; Williamson, 1967).
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>3. Incomplete mixing at the molecular level and close packing.</strong> Water and ethanol mix in
            all proportions, but they are not randomly distributed on the molecular scale. In concentrated alcohol–water
            solutions, neutron diffraction has shown water existing as small hydrogen-bonded strings and clusters in a
            "fluid" of close-packed alkyl groups (Dixit et al., 2002); a similar partial segregation, with both
            components forming their own percolating networks, has been observed in ethanol–water mixtures (Lenton et
            al., 2018). Simulations suggest that the ethyl groups align in parallel through hydrophobic interaction
            and pack better, contributing to the negative partial excess volume of ethanol (Banerjee et al., 2012).
        </p>
        <p>
            An older explanation assumed that water builds ordered, ice-like cages ("icebergs" or clathrate-like
            structures) around the alkyl group (Frank &amp; Evans, 1945). The idea stayed in textbooks for a long time;
            however, neutron diffraction studies found no direct evidence for such structures (Dixit et al., 2002),
            and simulations with polarisable models saw no extra hydrogen bonding right next to the alkyl group
            (Zhong &amp; Patel, 2009). The current view is that the contraction is the combined outcome of hydrogen
            bonding, hydrophobic hydration and partial clustering. Their shares change with composition: hydrophobic
            hydration dominates in the water-rich region, hydrogen bonding and clustering in the intermediate and
            alcohol-rich regions.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="oiml">
        <h2>The OIML R 22 equation</h2>
        <p>
            International Recommendation No. 22 "Alcoholometry" of the International Organization of Legal
            Metrology (OIML) was approved at the 4th International Conference of Legal Metrology in October 1972;
            its first edition appeared in 1973. The "International Alcoholometric Tables" based on it were
            published in 1975 by the International Bureau of Legal Metrology (BIML) (OIML, 1975). The tables rest
            on a single equation giving the density of a water–ethanol mixture as a function of mass fraction and
            temperature:
        </p>
        <p class="rehber-formul">
            ρ(w, t) = A<sub>1</sub> + Σ<sub>k=2</sub><sup>12</sup> A<sub>k</sub> w<sup>k−1</sup>
            + Σ<sub>k=1</sub><sup>6</sup> B<sub>k</sub> (t − 20)<sup>k</sup>
            + Σ<sub>i=1</sub><sup>5</sup> Σ<sub>k=1</sub><sup>m<sub>i</sub></sup> C<sub>i,k</sub> w<sup>k</sup> (t − 20)<sup>i</sup>
        </p>
        <p>
            ρ is the density in kg/m³, w the mass fraction (0–1) and t the Celsius temperature (IPTS-68 scale). The
            first sum gives the density at 20 °C as a function of composition; A<sub>1</sub> = 998.20123 kg/m³ is
            the density of water at 20 °C. The second sum describes how pure water changes with temperature and the
            third the interaction of composition and temperature; the upper limits of the inner sums are
            m = (11, 10, 9, 4, 2). The equation is valid from −20 °C to +40 °C. At 20 °C it gives 789.24 kg/m³ for
            w = 1 (pure ethanol; the value in the OIML errata) and 998.20 kg/m³ for w = 0 (water).
        </p>
        <p>
            The coefficients were fitted by least squares to five data sets: Kawasaki, Minowa and Inamatsu at the
            National Research Laboratory of Metrology (NRLM), Tokyo; Jaulmes and Brun Tep at the Faculty of
            Pharmacy, Montpellier; Osborne, McKelvy and Bearce at the National Bureau of Standards (NBS),
            Washington (Osborne et al., 1913); Mendeleev's measurements as reconsidered by Georgievskii; and
            Wagenbreth at the PTB in Germany. A degree of 12 was chosen for the composition polynomial because the
            standard deviation no longer improved at higher degrees (OIML, 1975).
        </p>
        <p>
            The subject is still being studied: Khattab et al. (2012) measured density, viscosity and surface
            tension between 293 and 323 K, and Danahy et al. (2018) examined in detail how to compute composition
            from measured density and temperature. In legal metrology, however, the reference remains the OIML
            equation.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="tablolar">
        <h2>The OIML tables</h2>
        <p>
            OIML R 22 contains a series of tables derived from the equation. The tool's three tabs correspond to
            them:
        </p>
        <div class="rehber-tablo-sarmal">
            <table class="rehber-tablo">
                <caption>Table 1. The OIML R 22 tables. p strength by mass (%), q strength by volume (%, 20 °C), t temperature, ρ<sub>20</sub> density at 20 °C.</caption>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Table</th>
                        <th scope="col">Given → found</th>
                        <th scope="col">How OIML computed it</th>
                        <th scope="col">Equivalent in the tool</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">I</th>
                        <td>p, t → ρ</td>
                        <td>Directly from the equation</td>
                        <td>Density from strength (% w/w)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">II</th>
                        <td>q, t → ρ</td>
                        <td>q → p by interpolation in IIIb, then the equation</td>
                        <td>Density from strength (% v/v)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">IIIa</th>
                        <td>p → ρ<sub>20</sub></td>
                        <td>Directly from the equation</td>
                        <td>Density from strength, 20 °C</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">IIIb</th>
                        <td>p → q</td>
                        <td>q = p · ρ<sub>20</sub>(p) / ρ<sub>20</sub>(100 %)</td>
                        <td>% w/w → % v/v conversion</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">IVa</th>
                        <td>q → ρ<sub>20</sub></td>
                        <td>q → p from IIIb, then the equation</td>
                        <td>Density from strength (% v/v), 20 °C</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">IVb</th>
                        <td>q → p</td>
                        <td>By interpolation in IIIb</td>
                        <td>% v/v → % w/w conversion</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">Va, Vb</th>
                        <td>ρ<sub>20</sub> → p, q</td>
                        <td>By interpolation in IIIa and IIIb</td>
                        <td>Strength from density, 20 °C</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">VI, VII (Annex I)</th>
