                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>
            So that the tables derived by interpolation would not spoil the two printed decimals, OIML required the
            calculations to be carried out with 16 significant digits and the interpolation to be made from an
            intermediate table at 0.1 % by mass intervals. Tables VIII–X, which correct glass alcoholometer and
            hydrometer readings for the expansion of the glass, are not part of this tool.
        </p>
        <p>
            The tool neither reads values from the tables nor interpolates: every result is computed directly from
            the equation. Where the equation has to be inverted (IVb, Va/Vb, VI/VII), the numerical methods
            described below are used.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="ters-cozum">
        <h2>The inverse solution: from strength by volume to strength by mass</h2>
        <p>
            Going from strength by mass to strength by volume is easy: once w is known, ρ(w, 20 °C) is computed and
            substituted into the φ relation above. The reverse requires solving an equation:
        </p>
        <p class="rehber-formul">
            w · ρ(w, 20 °C) = φ · ρ<sub>E</sub>(20 °C)
        </p>
        <p>
            Since ρ(w, 20 °C) is a polynomial of degree 11 in w, the left-hand side is a polynomial of degree 12. By
            the Abel–Ruffini theorem there is no general formula expressing the roots of polynomial equations of
            degree five or higher in terms of the coefficients using arithmetic operations and radicals. OIML
            therefore performed the conversion by interpolation between tables; the tool uses a numerical method.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Fixed-point iteration.</strong> The equation is written as w = g(w) = φ · ρ<sub>E</sub>(20) /
            ρ(w, 20) and w<sub>n+1</sub> = g(w<sub>n</sub>) is repeated starting from w<sub>0</sub> = φ. The method
            converges if |g′(w)| &lt; 1; here |g′(w)| = w · |dρ/dw| / ρ stays below 0.4 over the whole range (≈0.33
            around 96 % v/v). Each step therefore at least halves the error, and the iteration always converges.
            It stops when two successive values differ by less than 10<sup>−12</sup> in mass fraction.
        </p>
        <div class="rehber-tablo-sarmal">
            <table class="rehber-tablo">
                <caption>Table 2. Iteration steps for φ = 70 % v/v. The result converges to 62.386109 % w/w in 15 steps; with the same criterion 10 % v/v takes 7 steps, 40 % takes 11 and 96 % takes 22.</caption>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">n</th>
                        <th scope="col">w<sub>n</sub> (% w/w)</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><th scope="row">0</th><td>70.0000 (start: w<sub>0</sub> = φ)</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>63.6769</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>62.5991</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>62.4211</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>62.3919</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">5</th><td>62.3871</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">…</th><td>…</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">15</th><td>62.386109</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>
            <strong>Strength from density: bisection.</strong> Finding the strength by mass from a measured density
            means solving ρ(w, t) = ρ<sub>measured</sub>. It was checked at 0.01 % steps that, at every temperature
            from −20 to 40 °C, density decreases steadily as w increases; the root is therefore unique. The tool
            searches for it on [0, 1] by bisection: if the density at the midpoint of the interval is higher than
            the measured one, the root lies in the right half, otherwise in the left half. After about 40 halvings
            the interval is narrower than 10<sup>−12</sup>. The w found is converted to strength by volume with the
            20 °C relation.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="seyreltme">
        <h2>Dilution calculation</h2>
        <p>
            In a dilution the conserved quantity is the mass of ethanol. With m<sub>stock</sub> the mass of stock,
            m<sub>final</sub> the mass of the mixture to be prepared and w<sub>stock</sub>, w<sub>final</sub> the
            strengths by mass:
        </p>
        <p class="rehber-formul">
            m<sub>stock</sub> · w<sub>stock</sub> = m<sub>final</sub> · w<sub>final</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
            m<sub>water</sub> = m<sub>final</sub> − m<sub>stock</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
            m<sub>final</sub> = V<sub>final</sub> · ρ<sub>final</sub>(t)
        </p>
        <p>
            When the target is given by volume, the strengths are first converted to strengths by mass and the
            required volume is turned into a mass using the density of the mixture at the preparation temperature.
            Once the masses of stock and water are known, their volumes are computed from their own densities at the
            same temperature. The mass balance holds at any temperature. For a dilution by volume prepared at
            20 °C the stock volume equals the one given by simple proportion (V · φ<sub>target</sub> /
            φ<sub>stock</sub>), because the volume of ethanol at 20 °C is conserved by definition; the water
            required, however, is more than simple proportion suggests, because of contraction.
        </p>
        <div class="rehber-tablo-sarmal">
            <table class="rehber-tablo">
                <caption>Table 3. Examples of % v/v dilutions at 20 °C (from the tool). "Simple proportion" takes the water as the target volume minus the stock volume; contraction is the amount by which the stock and water volumes together exceed the target volume.</caption>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Stock → target</th>
                        <th scope="col">Amount</th>
                        <th scope="col">Stock</th>
                        <th scope="col">Water (correct)</th>
                        <th scope="col">Water (simple proportion)</th>
                        <th scope="col">Contraction</th>
                        <th scope="col">Final mass</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><th scope="row">96 % → 70 %</th><td>100 mL</td><td>72.92 mL (58.87 g)</td><td>29.74 mL (29.68 g)</td><td>27.08 mL</td><td>2.65 mL</td><td>88.56 g</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">96 % → 50 %</th><td>100 mL</td><td>52.08 mL (42.05 g)</td><td>51.05 mL (50.96 g)</td><td>47.92 mL</td><td>3.14 mL</td><td>93.01 g</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">90 % → 70 %</th><td>100 mL</td><td>77.78 mL (64.49 g)</td><td>24.11 mL (24.06 g)</td><td>22.22 mL</td><td>1.89 mL</td><td>88.56 g</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">80 % → 60 %</th><td>100 mL</td><td>75.00 mL (64.45 g)</td><td>26.51 mL (26.47 g)</td><td>25.00 mL</td><td>1.51 mL</td><td>90.91 g</td></tr>
                    <tr><th scope="row">96 % → 70 %</th><td>50 mL</td><td>36.46 mL (29.44 g)</td><td>14.87 mL (14.84 g)</td><td>13.54 mL</td><td>1.33 mL</td><td>44.28 g</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>
            <strong>Why simple proportion is not enough.</strong> To make 100 mL of 70 % alcohol from 96 %, if
            27.08 mL of water is added to 72.92 mL of stock by simple proportion, the mixture comes to about 97.5 mL
            and its strength is about 71.8 % instead of 70 %. This is why pharmacists often follow a "prepare 10 %
            extra" rule: for 50 mL, for example, the calculation is done for 55 mL and the surplus discarded. That
            closes the volume gap but does not correct the strength. The correct amount of water (here 29.74 mL)
            brings both the volume and the strength to target, and nothing extra needs to be prepared.
        </p>
        <p>
            When the target is given by mass (% w/w, amount in g), the calculation is a plain mass balance; density
            and temperature are used only to compute the volumes shown for information.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="sicaklik">
        <h2>Temperature</h2>
        <p>
            Strength by volume is defined with respect to volumes at 20 °C; 70 % v/v alcohol is 70 % once brought to
            20 °C, whatever the temperature at which it was prepared. Volumes measured with glassware, however, are
            volumes at the temperature of measurement. The tool therefore computes the stock, water and mixture
            volumes from densities at the <strong>preparation temperature</strong>. For example, making 100 mL of
            70 % alcohol from 96 % at 25 °C takes 72.96 mL (58.60 g) of stock and 29.63 mL (29.54 g) of water, with a
            final mass of 88.14 g. The difference from the 20 °C values is small, but because concentrated alcohol
            expands markedly more than water it grows as the temperature moves away from 20 °C.
        </p>
        <p>
            Masses do not depend on temperature. The most reliable approach is therefore to <strong>prepare by
            weighing</strong>: the tool gives the mass alongside the volume at every step. Adding the stock alcohol
            and the water by weight into a tared vessel removes both the reading error of the measuring vessel and
            the effect of temperature. The thermal expansion of the glassware itself is not taken into account.
        </p>
        <p>
            Mixing releases heat (Boyne &amp; Williamson, 1967). In the small volumes of a prescription the heat
            dissipates quickly and its effect on the measurement is negligible. In large volumes (for example when
            a stock is prepared at the litre scale), however, the mixture warms noticeably and cools slowly, and a
            warm mixture looks larger than it is. In that case read the final volume only after the mixture has
            returned to the preparation temperature. Preparing by weight avoids this problem.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="kullanim">
        <h2>Using the tool</h2>
        <p>
            The <a href="/alcohol-dilution">Alcohol Dilution</a> tool has three tabs.
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Dilution recipe.</strong> Enter the stock strength, the target strength, the amount to
                prepare and the preparation temperature (−20…40 °C), and choose % v/v (amount in mL) or % w/w (amount
                in g) as the basis. The target must be greater than 0 and lower than the stock, and the stock at most
                100. The result is a step-by-step recipe: the volume and mass of stock to take, the volume and mass of
                water to add, the final mass, the volume lost on mixing and the water volume simple proportion would
                have suggested.</li>
            <li><strong>Density from strength.</strong> Enter a strength (% v/v or % w/w) and a temperature; the
                density of the mixture is given in kg/m³ and g/mL, together with the strength on the other basis
                (% v/v ↔ % w/w). This tab corresponds to OIML Tables I, II, IIIa, IIIb, IVa and IVb.</li>
            <li><strong>Strength from density.</strong> Enter a measured true density and the measurement
                temperature; the strength by mass (% w/w) and the strength by volume at 20 °C (% v/v) are given. At
                20 °C the result corresponds to Tables Va/Vb, at other temperatures to Tables VI/VII of Annex I. The
                density must lie between the densities of pure ethanol and pure water at that temperature. Example:
                885.00 kg/m³ at 20 °C → 62.63 % w/w, 70.23 % v/v; the same density measured at 25 °C → 60.83 % w/w,
                68.54 % v/v.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            The strength-from-density tab does not correct glass alcoholometer or hydrometer readings (OIML Tables
            VIII–X); the value entered must be a true density, obtained for example with an oscillating U-tube
            density meter or a pycnometer.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="karsilastirma">
        <h2>Comparison with the printed tables</h2>
        <p>
            The tool was compared with the printed tables of OIML R 22. The tables were read from scanned pages into
            a test fixture; reading (OCR) errors were removed without using the calculation code, relying only on the
            internal consistency of each table row. The fixture contains 6 889 cells from 8 tables; in 6 884 of them
            the code's result rounded to two decimals equals the printed value. In the remaining 5 cells the
            difference is 0.01, and every one of them lies exactly on a rounding boundary (third decimal 5).
        </p>
