    <section id="sinirlar">
        <h2>Limitations</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Water–ethanol only.</strong> The equation is for a binary mixture. In tinctures and extracts,
                dissolved solids raise the density, and the strength derived from density departs from the true
                alcohol content; for such samples the alcohol should first be separated by distillation, or a
                correction for the dry residue applied (Bernatonienė et al., 2003). Congeners such as methanol and
                fusel oils can also shift the strength by 0.2–0.4 points (Kolesnov et al., 2023).</li>
            <li><strong>No hydrometer correction.</strong> The glass expansion correction needed for glass
                alcoholometer and hydrometer readings (Tables VIII–X) is not applied; the input must be a true
                density.</li>
            <li><strong>Freezing at low temperatures.</strong> Low-strength mixtures freeze at low temperatures, which
                is why OIML Table I starts, at each temperature, from the minimum permissible strength. The equation
                still returns a number in that region, but it does not correspond to a liquid mixture.</li>
            <li><strong>Pressure.</strong> The equation applies at normal atmospheric pressure.</li>
            <li><strong>Injectable solutions.</strong> The tool dilutes the stock with water. For ethanol-containing
                injections the diluent should be 0.9 % NaCl rather than water, otherwise the hypotonic mixture may
                cause haemolysis (Serdons et al., 2008). Because the density of normal saline differs slightly from
                that of water, the calculation is then approximate.</li>
            <li><strong>Practice note.</strong> 70 % alcohol used for ethanol-lock therapy has been reported to remain
                stable for 14 days in polypropylene syringes (Cober &amp; Johnson, 2007); storage conditions should be
                assessed separately.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="tarihce-uygulama" class="rehber-uygulama">
        <h2><i class="ti ti-tool" aria-hidden="true"></i> History and implementation on this site</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>The history of this tool.</strong> The tool is the successor of a Windows desktop program
            (WinForms) by the author (Mehmet Ali Ege). The program was written because pharmacists diluting by
            simple proportion always ended up with less alcohol than expected; the author also built an
            Arduino-based dilution device working on the same calculation. In the desktop version the conversion
            between strength by volume and by mass used a 4th-degree polynomial fitted in Excel; its error reached
            ±0.12 points (% w/w) over 0–96 % and −0.36 points at 100 %. In the web version that approximate
            polynomial has been replaced by the OIML R 22 equation itself.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Equation:</strong> <code>PTCalc.Core/Core/Alcoholometry/OimlAlcoholometry.cs</code>, ρ(w, t)
                with the OIML R 22 coefficients; valid from −20 to 40 °C.</li>
            <li><strong>% v/v → % w/w:</strong> fixed-point iteration, w<sub>0</sub> = φ, tolerance 10<sup>−12</sup>
                (mass fraction).</li>
            <li><strong>Density → strength:</strong> bisection on [0, 1], tolerance 10<sup>−12</sup>; an error message
                if the density lies outside the ethanol and water densities at that temperature.</li>
            <li><strong>Dilution:</strong> <code>PTCalc.Core/Core/Alcoholometry/AlcoholDilution.cs</code>, ethanol mass
                balance; volumes from densities at the preparation temperature; contraction = V<sub>stock</sub> +
                V<sub>water</sub> − V<sub>final</sub>.</li>
            <li><strong>Comparison:</strong> <code>PTCalc.Core.Tests/AlcoholometryTests.cs</code> and
                <code>oiml_r22_reference.json</code> (6 889 printed table cells); plus unit tests for the round trip of
                the conversions, the density-to-strength solution at every temperature and the dilution mass
                balance.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

